	"identifying_description": "Weathervane pattern with cockerel",
	"web_text": "Since the mid-nineteenth century, mola (“cloth”) have been the cultural markers of the Kuna, an indigenous people of Panama and Colombia. Hand sewn in pairs, the panels are affixed to the fronts and backs of blouses, forming their main decorative interest. The reverse appliqué technique, in which outlines are cut from the top fabric to expose lower layers, is further enhanced by embroidery. The patterns found in mola of the early to mid-twentieth century, such as the weathervane motif seen here, reveal increased contact with modern society during this period.",
	"didactic_label": "Since the mid-nineteenth century, mola (“cloth”) have been the cultural markers of the Kuna, an indigenous people of Panama and Colombia. Hand sewn in pairs, the panels are affixed to the fronts and backs of blouses, forming their main decorative interest. The reverse appliqué technique, in which outlines are cut from the top fabric to expose lower layers, is further enhanced by embroidery. The patterns found in mola of the early to mid-twentieth century, such as the weathervane motif seen here, reveal increased contact with modern society during this period.",
